-> **The one distinction worth learning first.** A pipeline **definition** is
configuration — where to check out, where the config file is. A pipeline **run**
is one execution of a definition, and it spawns workflows and jobs. Terraform
manages definitions and reads runs. The
[object model](guides/object-model) guide covers this properly, because getting
it backwards is the most common source of confusion.

## CircleCI Server

```terraform
provider "circleci" {
  host        = "https://circleci.example.com"
  deployment  = "server"
  runner_host = "https://circleci.example.com"
}
```

## Authentication

The provider authenticates with a CircleCI [personal access
token](https://circleci.com/docs/managing-api-tokens/). Supply it with the
`CIRCLE_TOKEN` environment variable rather than in configuration, so it is not
committed to source control:

```shell
export CIRCLE_TOKEN="..."
```

Some resources need a token belonging to a user with organization admin
permissions. Notably, creating a `user-key` checkout key requires a personal
token rather than a project token.

~> **The token is a person's.** CircleCI has no organization-scoped API token, so
whatever runs `terraform apply` holds one specific human's credential: it stops
working when they leave, and every change Terraform makes is attributed to them
in the audit log. Plan for a dedicated machine user rather than discovering this
later.

## Environment variables

Every provider attribute may be set with an environment variable. A value set in
configuration takes precedence.

| Attribute | Environment variable | Default |
|---|---|---|
| `host` | `CIRCLE_HOST` | `https://circleci.com` |
| `key` | `CIRCLE_TOKEN` | — (required) |
| `runner_host` | `CIRCLE_RUNNER_HOST` | `https://runner.circleci.com` |
| `deployment` | `CIRCLE_DEPLOYMENT` | `cloud` |

## CircleCI Cloud and CircleCI Server

The `deployment` attribute tells the provider which kind of installation `host`
refers to, and it selects the API version used for each resource.

A resource that is unavailable on Server reports an explicit error rather than a
confusing HTTP 404.

**The API version is not what decides availability — whether a Server
installation's gateway forwards the route is.** Some paths are forwarded to their
backend and some are not, independent of whether the path is v2 or v3. So a
route is available there when its backend is both deployed and reachable through
the gateway, whatever version the path carries. All four combinations occur:

| | Available on Server | Not available |
|---|---|---|
| **v2** | `circleci_url_orb_allow_list_entry` — served directly, not proxied elsewhere | `circleci_pipeline_definition` — the gateway does not forward `pipeline-definitions`; `circleci_otel_exporter` — owned by a service Server does not deploy |
| **v3** | the `circleci_runner_*` types — `/api/v3/runner` is explicitly forwarded to the runner service | `circleci_orb`, `circleci_ios_signing_certificate` — no matching v3 route exists |

So "it is v2" is not evidence of availability, and "it is v3" is not evidence
against it. Two further reasons are unrelated to routing at all:
`circleci_audit_log_config` is gated on a Cloud billing plan tier, and the group
types need a `circleci` type (standalone) organization, which a Server
installation never is. Each type's own page gives its actual reason.

### Which document says what

Three places describe availability, and they answer different questions. Where
they overlap, the narrower one wins:

| Document | Authoritative for | Scope |
|---|---|---|
| Each type's own `## Availability` table | **That one type, on the Cloud/Server axis** — plus its API routes, organization-type requirement and token requirement | One page, next to the schema it describes |
| The [compatibility matrix in the README](https://github.com/CircleCI-Public/terraform-provider-circleci#compatibility) | **The VCS-integration axis** — GitHub App vs GitHub OAuth vs GitLab vs Bitbucket Cloud vs GitHub Enterprise Server | Every type, per integration |
| The table below | **Nothing.** It is a summary and must never disagree with either | Grouped by area |

So: if this page and a type's own page disagree, the type's page is right and
this one is a bug — please report it.

